    { title: "Pick the Nano (or size up)", body: "Start at $6.49/mo with the Nano, or choose a bigger plan. Every tier includes the same dedicated IP, backups, and DDoS protection." },
    { title: "Deploy in about a minute", body: "Choose a one-click OS or app image and hit deploy — median provision time is around 47 seconds." },
    { title: "Scale only when you need to", body: "One-click resize adds vCPU, RAM, or disk without a rebuild, so you can start cheap and grow into it later." },
  ],
  specNote:
    "The $6.49 Nano handles a small site, a bot, a dev box, or a personal service comfortably. Jump to Starter (2 GB) or Standard (4 GB) when you need more headroom — and resize any time without a rebuild.",
  faq: [
    { q: "What's the cheapest plan, and is it actually usable?", a: "The Nano is $6.49/mo with 1 vCPU, 1 GB RAM, 15 GB NVMe, and 1 TB transfer. It's a full-root server with a dedicated IP and daily backups included — plenty for a small site, a bot, a dev environment, or a lightweight service." },
    { q: "Are there hidden fees on top of the sticker price?", a: "No. Pricing is all-in and monthly. The dedicated IPv4, DDoS protection, managed firewall, daily backups, and snapshots are already included, and there are no surprise egress or add-on bills." },
    { q: "Why is the intro price lower than the renewal price?", a: "New plans get an introductory discount off the first term, then renew at the regular monthly rate. The renewal price is shown up front on the pricing page — no bait and switch." },
    { q: "Does cheap mean slow or oversold hardware?", a: "No. Every plan runs on enterprise NVMe SSD over a self-healing ZFS mirror with dedicated vCPU allocation, backed by a 99.9% uptime SLA. The price is low; the hardware isn't." },
